introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we structure data so it tells the truth?"
  overview: "This unit explores <strong>Data Integrity</strong> and <strong>Relational Databases</strong>. You will learn to move beyond messy 'flat files' to structured <strong>Entities</strong> linked by <strong>Keys</strong>. The goal is to design systems that reduce redundancy (repetition) and ensure consistency."
  journey: "You'll start by deconstructing messy data into <strong>Normalized</strong> tables (1NF, 2NF, 3NF). Then you'll model these structures using <strong>ERD Diagrams</strong>, and finally, learn the language of databases — <strong>SQL</strong> — to query and manipulate the data."

concepts:
  - title: "The Relational Model"
    icon: "fa-database"
    content: |
      <p>Modern databases store data in related tables, not single spreadsheet files.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Entity (Table):</strong> A collection of data about one thing (e.g., "Students").</li>
          <li><strong>Tuple (Row):</strong> A single record (e.g., "John Doe").</li>
          <li><strong>Attribute (Column):</strong> A specific piece of data (e.g., "DateOfBirth").</li>
          <li><strong>Primary Key:</strong> A unique ID that identifies a record.</li>
          <li><strong>Foreign Key:</strong> A link to a Primary Key in another table.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Normalization"
    icon: "fa-filter"
    content: |
      <p>Normalization is a 3-step mathematical process to organize data and reduce redundancy:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>1NF:</strong> Atomic Data (no lists in cells) + Unique Rows.</li>
          <li><strong>2NF:</strong> 1NF + No Partial Dependencies (all attributes depend on the WHOLE key).</li>
          <li><strong>3NF:</strong> 2NF + No Transitive Dependencies (attributes depend ONLY on the key).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "ERD Diagrams"
    icon: "fa-project-diagram"
    content: |
      <p>We visualize database schemas using <strong>Entity Relationship Diagrams</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Entity:</strong> Represented by a rectangle.</li>
          <li><strong>Attribute:</strong> Ovals or listed inside.</li>
          <li><strong>Relationships:</strong> Lines connecting entities with <strong>Cardinality</strong> (1:1, 1:Many, Many:Many).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "SQL (Declarative Programming)"
    icon: "fa-code"
    content: |
      <p>SQL is not procedural (how to do it), it is declarative (what you want). You just ask the database:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>DDL (Data Definition):</strong> <code>CREATE</code>, <code>DROP</code> tables.</li>
          <li><strong>DML (Data Manipulation):</strong> <code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Database"
    definition: "An organized collection of data, generally stored and accessed electronically from a computer system."
  - term: "Relational Database"
    definition: "A type of database that stores and provides access to data points that are related to one another."
  - term: "Primary Key"
    definition: "A specific choice of a minimal set of attributes (columns) that uniquely specify a tuple (row) in a relation (table)."
  - term: "Foreign Key"
    definition: "A field (or collection of fields) in one table that refers to the PRIMARY KEY in another table."
  - term: "Normalization"
    definition: "The process of organizing data in a database to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity."
  - term: "Schema"
    definition: "The skeleton structure that represents the logical configuration of all or part of a relational database."
  - term: "Cardinality"
    definition: "Refers to the uniqueness of data values contained in a column (e.g., One-to-One, One-to-Many)."
  - term: "SQL"
    definition: "Structured Query Language. A domain-specific language used in programming for managing data held in a relational database management system."
  - term: "DDL"
    definition: "Data Definition Language. The subset of SQL used to define the data structures (e.g., CREATE TABLE)."
  - term: "DML"
    definition: "Data Manipulation Language. The subset of SQL used to manage data (e.g., SELECT, INSERT)."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Modality vs Cardinality"
    correction: "Cardinality is the MAXIMUM number (how many CAN exist). Modality was the MINIMUM (MUST it exist?)."
  - mistake: "Normalization vs Sorting"
    correction: "Normalization isn't just 'tidying up' or sorting rows. It's about splitting tables to remove logical dependencies."
  - mistake: "Object vs Table"
    correction: "A Class in OOP is NOT the same as a Table. Tables don't have methods, and they rely on Foreign Keys, not pointers."

